1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:02,125
A crowd at a bus stop.

2
00:00:02,125 --> 00:00:03,281
A cricket team.

3
00:00:03,281 --> 00:00:04,946
A machine in a factory.

4
00:00:05,346 --> 00:00:08,000
In a crowd, everyone does their own thing.

5
00:00:08,000 --> 00:00:10,236
In a team, players share the work.

6
00:00:10,236 --> 00:00:12,989
In a machine, every
part has one fixed job.

7
00:00:13,439 --> 00:00:18,038
Aarav noticed this in the college
corridor, and asked: do an animal's cells

8
00:00:18,038 --> 00:00:20,946
work like a crowd, a team, or a machine?

9
00:00:21,296 --> 00:00:22,988
It depends on the animal.

10
00:00:22,988 --> 00:00:27,294
Every animal has many cells, but
they are not organised the same way.

11
00:00:27,794 --> 00:00:32,386
By the end, you will know the four
levels of organisation, and two body

12
00:00:32,386 --> 00:00:34,416
systems exams love to compare.

13
00:00:35,066 --> 00:00:37,117
NCERT describes four levels.

14
00:00:37,467 --> 00:00:38,801
The cellular level.

15
00:00:38,801 --> 00:00:42,031
Cells work loosely, side
by side, like a crowd.

16
00:00:42,481 --> 00:00:43,778
The tissue level.

17
00:00:43,778 --> 00:00:47,637
Cells doing the same job join
into tissues, like a team.

18
00:00:48,087 --> 00:00:49,350
The organ level.

19
00:00:49,350 --> 00:00:53,088
Tissues group into organs,
each for one particular job.

20
00:00:53,538 --> 00:00:55,081
The organ-system level.

21
00:00:55,081 --> 00:00:58,976
Organs link up into systems,
like the parts of a machine.

22
00:00:59,626 --> 00:01:02,395
Let's zoom into a sponge,
the simplest case.

23
00:01:02,745 --> 00:01:05,483
From outside, a sponge is a hollow body.

24
00:01:05,483 --> 00:01:10,244
Water enters through tiny pores, the
ostia, and leaves through the osculum.

25
00:01:10,744 --> 00:01:12,173
Zoom into the wall.

26
00:01:12,173 --> 00:01:14,722
The cells sit loosely, side by side.

27
00:01:14,722 --> 00:01:17,430
There are no true tissues and no organs.

28
00:01:17,930 --> 00:01:18,830
Zoom again.

29
00:01:18,793 --> 00:01:21,250
This is a collar cell, or choanocyte.

30
00:01:21,250 --> 00:01:24,954
Its whip, the flagellum, beats
and keeps the water moving.

31
00:01:25,454 --> 00:01:27,626
Different cells do different jobs.

32
00:01:27,626 --> 00:01:30,802
This sharing of work is
called division of labour.

33
00:01:30,802 --> 00:01:32,983
But the cells never form tissues.

34
00:01:32,983 --> 00:01:34,817
That is the cellular level.

35
00:01:35,467 --> 00:01:38,905
Now climb the four levels,
with the NCERT examples.

36
00:01:39,255 --> 00:01:40,155
Step one.

37
00:01:40,098 --> 00:01:41,250
Cellular level.

38
00:01:41,250 --> 00:01:44,840
Loose groups of cells, with
some division of labour.

39
00:01:44,840 --> 00:01:46,197
Example: sponges.

40
00:01:46,947 --> 00:01:47,847
Step two.

41
00:01:47,811 --> 00:01:48,885
Tissue level.

42
00:01:48,885 --> 00:01:52,403
Cells doing the same job
are arranged into tissues.

43
00:01:52,403 --> 00:01:54,973
Example: coelenterates, like Hydra.

44
00:01:55,723 --> 00:01:56,689
Step three.

45
00:01:56,689 --> 00:01:57,707
Organ level.

46
00:01:57,707 --> 00:02:02,230
Tissues group together into organs,
each one for a particular job.

47
00:02:02,230 --> 00:02:05,053
Example: the flatworms, Platyhelminthes.

48
00:02:05,803 --> 00:02:06,760
Step four.

49
00:02:06,760 --> 00:02:08,157
Organ-system level.

50
00:02:08,157 --> 00:02:12,558
Organs work together as systems,
each with one body function.

51
00:02:12,558 --> 00:02:17,475
Examples: annelids, arthropods,
molluscs, echinoderms and chordates.

52
00:02:18,225 --> 00:02:20,706
Each level is built from the one before.

53
00:02:21,356 --> 00:02:22,487
Remember this.

54
00:02:22,487 --> 00:02:27,413
Cells make tissues, tissues make
organs, and organs make organ systems.

55
00:02:28,013 --> 00:02:30,681
Organ systems come in different designs.

56
00:02:30,681 --> 00:02:32,879
Start with the digestive system.

57
00:02:33,229 --> 00:02:35,714
A flatworm's gut has just one opening.

58
00:02:35,714 --> 00:02:39,620
Food goes in, and waste comes
out, through the same hole.

59
00:02:39,620 --> 00:02:42,145
This is an incomplete digestive system.

60
00:02:42,595 --> 00:02:47,136
A roundworm or an earthworm has a
tube with two openings: a mouth at the

61
00:02:47,136 --> 00:02:49,255
front and an anus at the back.

62
00:02:49,255 --> 00:02:51,609
This is a complete digestive system.

63
00:02:52,059 --> 00:02:56,710
In a tube, food moves one way, so
each part does one job after another,

64
00:02:56,710 --> 00:02:58,154
like a factory line.

65
00:02:58,804 --> 00:03:00,687
Now the circulatory system.

66
00:03:00,687 --> 00:03:02,847
Blood has to reach every cell.

67
00:03:03,197 --> 00:03:07,411
In the open type, the heart pumps
blood out, and the cells and tissues

68
00:03:07,411 --> 00:03:09,110
are bathed in it directly.

69
00:03:09,110 --> 00:03:10,698
Example: the arthropods.

70
00:03:11,148 --> 00:03:15,876
In the closed type, the blood always
stays inside vessels: arteries, veins

71
00:03:15,876 --> 00:03:17,036
and capillaries.

72
00:03:17,036 --> 00:03:20,436
Examples: the annelids and
the chordates, like us.

73
00:03:20,886 --> 00:03:23,642
Open is like water poured across a field.

74
00:03:23,642 --> 00:03:26,290
Closed is like water sent through pipes.

75
00:03:26,940 --> 00:03:27,913
Your turn.

76
00:03:27,913 --> 00:03:30,162
A honey bee is an arthropod.

77
00:03:30,162 --> 00:03:32,747
Is its circulation open or closed?

78
00:03:32,747 --> 00:03:34,180
Pause and decide.

79
00:03:37,380 --> 00:03:38,280
Open!

80
00:03:37,946 --> 00:03:40,741
Arthropods have an open
circulatory system.

81
00:03:40,741 --> 00:03:44,587
The blood leaves the heart and
bathes the tissues directly.

82
00:03:45,237 --> 00:03:48,524
Now, a worked example:
four animals on the levels.

83
00:03:48,874 --> 00:03:51,090
Spongilla, a freshwater sponge.

84
00:03:51,090 --> 00:03:52,094
Loose cells.

85
00:03:52,094 --> 00:03:53,254
Cellular level.

86
00:03:53,704 --> 00:03:54,604
Hydra.

87
00:03:54,389 --> 00:03:56,232
Its cells form tissues.

88
00:03:56,232 --> 00:03:57,378
Tissue level.

89
00:03:57,828 --> 00:04:00,522
Fasciola, the liver fluke, a flatworm.

90
00:04:00,522 --> 00:04:03,494
It has organs, and a gut with one opening.

91
00:04:03,494 --> 00:04:06,177
Organ level, incomplete digestive system.

92
00:04:06,627 --> 00:04:08,491
Pheretima, the earthworm.

93
00:04:08,491 --> 00:04:13,382
Full organ systems, a gut with two
openings, and blood inside vessels.

94
00:04:13,382 --> 00:04:17,268
Organ-system level, complete
digestion, closed circulation.

95
00:04:17,918 --> 00:04:19,384
Now, the exam trap.

96
00:04:19,734 --> 00:04:24,979
Many students think every animal at the
organ-system level has closed circulation.

97
00:04:25,379 --> 00:04:29,571
But arthropods are at the organ-system
level, and their circulation is

98
00:04:29,571 --> 00:04:30,471
open.

99
00:04:30,122 --> 00:04:34,778
The level tells how the organs are
grouped, not the type of circulation.

100
00:04:35,428 --> 00:04:36,723
Where is this asked?

101
00:04:36,723 --> 00:04:41,149
As statements that pair a group with
its level, its gut, or its circulation.

102
00:04:41,749 --> 00:04:42,967
Exam level now.

103
00:04:42,967 --> 00:04:44,763
Read the four statements.

104
00:04:44,763 --> 00:04:46,457
Which ones are correct?

105
00:04:46,457 --> 00:04:47,726
Pause and solve.

106
00:04:53,126 --> 00:04:54,437
The answer is A.

107
00:04:54,437 --> 00:04:56,206
Statements one and three.

108
00:04:56,706 --> 00:04:57,734
Two is wrong.

109
00:04:57,734 --> 00:05:01,448
Coelenterates are at the tissue
level, not the organ level.

110
00:05:01,948 --> 00:05:03,135
Four is wrong.

111
00:05:03,135 --> 00:05:06,852
Blood that stays only in
vessels is closed circulation.

112
00:05:06,852 --> 00:05:10,075
In open circulation,
blood bathes the tissues.

113
00:05:10,675 --> 00:05:12,137
Here is your recap.

114
00:05:12,137 --> 00:05:13,640
Screenshot this card.

115
00:05:16,840 --> 00:05:19,676
Can you answer these three
questions without help?

116
00:05:20,076 --> 00:05:20,976
One.

117
00:05:20,561 --> 00:05:23,110
What is division of labour in a sponge?

118
00:05:24,010 --> 00:05:24,910
Two.

119
00:05:24,564 --> 00:05:27,345
What makes a digestive system complete?

120
00:05:28,245 --> 00:05:29,145
Three.

121
00:05:28,907 --> 00:05:32,850
How is open circulation different
from closed circulation?

122
00:05:33,950 --> 00:05:38,266
Next time: cut a starfish, a
cockroach and a sponge with one line.

123
00:05:38,266 --> 00:05:40,657
Which ones split into matching halves?

124
00:05:40,657 --> 00:05:43,553
We find out in body
symmetry and germ layers.
